{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s groundbreaking policy to remove penalties for low-level drug ownership ends this weekend, reverting to criminal charges for personal drug possession under a fresh legal framework. The incoming measure, known as HB4002, shifts focus to enforcement while offering those found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine either a criminal charge or a pathway to rehabilitation. The new system allows for behavioral health programs and \"deflection\" options instead of fines, though qualifying for these alternatives requires meeting strict conditions—such as having no prior offenses, outstanding warrants, or violent history, and being medically stable. Possession for personal use will now be classified as a minor offense, carrying penalties of up to six months in detention.\n\nSupporters argue the update simplifies law enforcement efforts to address public drug use while intensifying consequences for drug distribution near high-traffic areas like recreational spaces. Counties have the flexibility—but no obligation—to design their own diversion programs, tailoring approaches to local capacity. Critics warn this flexibility could lead to inconsistent enforcement, raising concerns about fairness across regions.\n\nUnder the new rules, eligible individuals will trigger a process where officers relay details to a county-run deflection team, which must respond within half an hour to connect them with mobile outreach services. If the outreach team fails to arrive in that window, authorities will proceed with arrest rather than waiting.\n\n***P.P.S.*** While drug policies shift, everyday routines—like grocery shopping or savoring a meal—remain tied to societal norms shaped by an individual’s experiences and the broader trends influencing the nation’s p